The isolated compiler identity fix now supports a real continuation through the
compiler output. The seed and all eleven follow-up cases passed: **12/12** tasks,
**19 actual action executions** including the seed, and **25 fresh JSON service
processes**. The consumer resolves candidate and observation records by the IDs
returned from the native compiler before selecting an action. It then executes
the action and checks actual output independently.

The original `development-v1` matrix, adverse timings and 11/11 compiler identity
mismatches are preserved byte for byte. Its artifact manifest covers 828 files.
`compiler-continuation-v1` uses the separately preregistered continuation protocol
and an explicit normal-import overlay under `source_profiles/compiler_identity_v1`.
All unchanged package modules resolve to the clean pinned base worktree; the
overlay copies ancestor package initializers and patches one adapter module.
There are no module stubs, production source edits or provider/model calls.

`DatasetsProgramWorldAdapter.cite_transition` originally chose `query_cid`
before candidate/prediction/observation identities. Caller inspection found the
four corresponding `ExecutionTransitionCompiler.compile` calls and an existing
test that checked only query identity. The isolated patch selects a record's own
identity from its closed transition schema. It also rejects ambiguous
unschematized multi-ID citation views. The exact patch is in
`source_profiles/compiler_identity_v1/source.diff`.

| Source | SHA-256 |
| --- | --- |
| Original adapter | `ac947158c66729fd63333cfebf82886a887ac7683e9a515a1f293357104e7788` |
| Isolated patched adapter | `a10e7c415228070b5fd6ff8e91a19bf75b6dda4ed95cde692b1e8d81f3759703` |

The service's `record` operation persists actual controller-declared execution
evidence as native transition records. `propose` retrieves those records,
applies native current-state admission and candidate binding, compiles an
unadmitted proposal, then retrieves the compiler-selected records by CID from
the native store. A wrong query CID in the candidate field would fail the
native record-kind lookup on this path. All seven admitted follow-up proposals
preserved distinct candidate/observation IDs and caused the selected action to
execute first. Changed source, state and environment each produced `stale` and
actual search fallback. Untrusted observation produced `abstain`; the next
observed replacement supported later consumption.

This continuation measured 17.801740 seconds across its 25 service subprocesses,
0.366345 seconds in 19 action subprocesses, and 18.297378 seconds across the
12 complete cases. These costs include separate service startup for proposing
and recording and are reported as integration overhead, not as a speedup
comparison with the differently structured original matrix.

The first identity regression attempt produced 20 passes and two failures:
full candidate mappings hit the pre-existing `rank` guard before citation.
The exact failing source and logs are retained in `tests/identity-v1/`; an
unchanged repeat retained complete output after the first display was truncated.
The final tests explicitly distinguish typed records/minimal public identity
views from unsupported full candidate mappings. They check six record types,
missing own IDs, ambiguous IDs, compiler link preservation and the isolated
source pins: **23/23 passed**, with logs and test source in `tests/identity-v2/`.
The prediction in the citation test is a typed contract fixture only; no learned
prediction was executed. The full candidate mapping restriction and ranks
beyond zero remain unqualified and are not hidden by the passing continuation.

`validate_continuation.py` independently checked original-artifact immutability,
prerun code hashes, actual imported module hashes, JSON operation receipts,
fresh service processes, current-binding changes, compiler-selected record IDs,
raw action outputs and required current execution: **1183/1183 checks passed**.
There were no failed service/continuation iterations. The results and all actual
SQLite/block artifacts remain in `runs/compiler-continuation-v1/`.

The JSON operation is documented in `SERVICE.md` and exported with pins in
`service_operation.json` for an integrated evaluation controller. Its authority
remains a local evaluation producer declaration, not signed runner evidence or
a native execution proof. The exact-query index, search policy, dispatcher and
task checker remain evaluation integration. This increment fixes a demonstrated
metadata defect and exercises compiler-mediated consumption; it does not show
learned world-memory benefits, cross-family transfer, production admission or
token savings. The original exact-retrieval/template parity and full-scope E05
gap remain unchanged.
